--- function body ---
export async function mintFileKeyOnRotate(node: Node, _job: RotationJobRecord): Promise<void> {
  if (!node.content) {
    // Folder node (or any node without content) — no-op.
    return;
  }
  const fileKeyPrime = generateRandomBytes(32);
  node.content.fileKey = fileKeyPrime;
}

=== contentRekeyPending references anywhere in sdk-core/core? ===
packages/sdk-core/src/rotation/engine.ts:194: * Phase 64 implementation: mint a fresh `fileKey'`, mark `contentRekeyPending`

=== 64-03 plan: contentRekeyPending in acceptance criteria? ===
15:    - "After rotation the file node is re-sealed under readKey' carrying fileKey'; a holder of the old readKey/fileKey cannot decrypt the next published version (§7.3 test 2)"
23:    - "Never add a contentRekeyPending field to @cipherbox/core NodeContent — the node/v3 schema is frozen this phase (lazy re-key wiring is Phase 65)"
28:Fill the `mintFileKeyOnRotate` seam (ROT-03 / CRIT-1) in `packages/sdk-core/src/rotation/engine.ts` — currently `throw new Error('not implemented — phase 64 (ROT-03/CRIT-1 content-key rotation)')` at L200-202. Per design §4.1 and ADR 0002 (D-05): rotating a file node must mint a fresh `fileKey' = 32 random bytes` so that read-revocation protects FUTURE content. Re-sealing the read-body under `readKey'` alone is insufficient — the revoked reader already holds the old `content.fileKey`; if the next write reuses it the revoked reader can still decrypt it (Pitfall 2).
30:Purpose: close CRIT-1, the highest-severity revocation gap. The re-key is lazy (applied on the next content write — Phase 65 wires the write path); Phase 64's job is to mint `fileKey'` and place it on the node so `rotateOne`'s `sealNode(updatedNode, readKeyPrime, …)` re-seals the body carrying the new fileKey. ADR 0002 honesty: a cold file never rewritten keeps its old still-pinned CID decryptable — read-revocation protects future writes/navigation/filenames, NOT already-distributed content. Crypto business logic with defined I/O → TDD.
52:    - packages/core/src/node/types.ts (L57-67 NodeContent — fileKey: Uint8Array; no contentRekeyPending field exists)
65:    Replace the `mintFileKeyOnRotate(_node, _job)` throw body. Strip the leading underscores from the params (signature frozen: `(node: Node, job: RotationJobRecord): Promise<void>`). Mint `const fileKeyPrime = generateRandomBytes(32)` (import from `@cipherbox/crypto`; do NOT hand-roll randomness). If `node.content` is present, set `node.content.fileKey = fileKeyPrime` so `rotateOne`'s subsequent `sealNode(updatedNode, readKeyPrime, …)` re-seals the read-body carrying the new fileKey. Do NOT add a `contentRekeyPending` schema field (frozen schema — the lazy re-encrypt-on-next-write wiring is Phase 65; the minted fileKey on the sealed body IS the Phase-64 deliverable). Do NOT zero `node.content.fileKey` after assignment (rotateOne consumes it via sealNode). Reference `void job` only if the param is unused (job-record persistence is Phase 68). Add the three behavior tests to `engine.test.ts` reusing the existing vi.hoisted mock harness; for Test 3 mock `sealNode` and assert the `updatedNode` argument carries `fileKeyPrime` in `content.fileKey`.
78:  <done>File rotation mints a fresh fileKey sealed under readKey'; old readKey/fileKey holder cannot decrypt the next published version; folder nodes unaffected; suite green.</done>
86:| pre-rotation fileKey holder → next published file version | reusing the old fileKey after read-revocation lets a revoked reader decrypt future content |

